module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.refused_admission_leaves_the_side_effect_it_ran_first

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundBelowTheLadder, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { StructurallyGuaranteed }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }

data refused_admission_leaves_the_side_effect_it_ran_first: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "refused_admission_leaves_the_side_effect_it_ran_first" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundBelowTheLadder,
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundBelowTheLadder,
      ceiling: StructurallyGuaranteed,
      blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt,
      trigger_capability: "the capability of testing a caller-supplied path against the admission predicate that governs it BEFORE any call that creates at that path, so that every refusal arm is a refusal that created nothing and a caller cannot observe an artifact the refusal said it would not have.",
    },
  },

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a gate asks whether a caller may do a thing only AFTER a call that does the thing has already run, so the refusal carries the meaning 'this was not allowed' while the filesystem, the process table, or the remote keeps the artifact the allowed case would also have produced. The refusal is correct and the world is not: the caller is told no, and something is left behind as if the answer had been yes.",

    "HARM: the artifact is created at a path the caller chose, which the refusing code had already decided it must not touch. A production path is the case that matters -- a refusal that says 'production is not yours' while leaving a directory in production has refused the wrong thing. The leak is also invisible: the return value of the refusing arm is identical whether or not anything was created, so no caller can check, and the residue is found by whoever later trips over a directory they did not make.",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS: any function of the shape create-then-check has it, and it hides in exactly the place review is good at -- each arm returns a typed, located refusal, so the error-handling is exemplary and passes inspection. The defect is not in the refusals; it is in the ORDER of the two calls, and order is invisible to a reader checking that every failure arm is loud. A fold over failure arms cannot see it, because every arm is correct at the moment it is chosen. The tell within one function is arms that disagree about whether refusal cleans up. Two look-alike rows are NOT this class, and the difference is where the effect sits relative to the check: gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.an_unreached_effect_leg_reads_as_one_that_ran_and_refused is about a refusal REPORTED for a leg that never ran (the residue claim is false, here it is true and unreported), and gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_killed_run_leaves_its_credential_file_behind is cleanup that is complete along every control-flow path and defeated by a signal (there the order is right and the process dies; here nothing dies, the code simply asked too late).",

    "SPECIMEN, found 2026-11-17 by dashboard review 96213 on PR #16646, in the wet carrier fixture at test.claim.roadmap.roadmap_event_carrier_fixture::roadmap_event_carrier_mint_scratch. That mint accepts an unrestricted template, passes it to shell.Mktemp.DirWithTemplate, and only THEN asks roadmap_carrier_root_is_minted_scratch whether the directory it now has is scratch this file may own. The shape arm returns RoadmapEventCarrierMintRefused { detail: 'the directory minted at ' + path } without removing anything -- unlike its sibling seal-planting arm, which does reclaim what it made and reports when that reclamation fails. So a caller passing a template outside /tmp gets a refusal that is entirely correct, and a directory that should not exist. The two arms disagreeing about whether refusal cleans up is the tell: one author asked 'what do I report', the other also asked 'what do I leave'.",

    "WHY IT SURVIVES TESTING. The checks that exist are armed on the path the checked-in callers take, and those callers pass /tmp templates, so the check-after-create arm is reached only with an input whose creation was already harmless. A test suite that never passes a template the gate would refuse cannot distinguish 'refusal that created nothing' from 'refusal that created something', and both look identical in the return value. The bug is reachable only by an input nobody was authorized to pass -- which is also the definition of the caller who will report it as a leak.",

    "WHY THE FIX IS ORDER, NOT A WIDER CHECK OR A RETRY. A second check, or a cleanup that runs after the fact, both leave a window in which the artifact exists, and both depend on a later step being remembered. The ceiling is structural: ask the predicate first, so there is no moment at which the unauthorized artifact exists, and the refusal arms need no cleanup logic at all because they have nothing to clean. When the creation itself is the only way to learn the answer, the repair is containment instead -- create under a path already established as disposable, and treat any post-creation refusal as a cleanup obligation whose failure is itself a reported error, exactly as the seal-planting arm of the specimen does.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE: for every refusal this codebase can return, name the call that ran before it and ask what that call put into the world. If the answer is 'nothing', the refusal is safe to return unqualified. If it is not nothing, the refusal must either prove it undid what it did, or say in its own text what it left behind -- and a comment that defers that proof is the stall this row exists to prevent, which is why the prose in the specimen cites THIS row rather than a promise about a future pull request.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: below the ladder. The class is discovered and named, no admitted code prevents the ordering, and an author can honour it by hand -- one arm of the specimen already does, which is what makes it mitigatable rather than merely documented.",

    "CEILING: structurally guaranteed, by a gate that evaluates the admission predicate BEFORE the creating call and therefore has no refusal arm with an artifact to account for. BLOCKER: that capability is unbuilt, not unavailable -- nothing about the template check requires new reach, only that the check move ahead of the creation.",

    "NEXT TRIGGER: the follow-up behaviour change to test.claim.roadmap.roadmap_event_carrier_fixture::roadmap_event_carrier_mint_scratch, which moves the admission predicate ahead of shell.Mktemp.DirWithTemplate (or reclaims on refusal) and carries a red control. The two repairs are NOT equivalent and the row says so: rejecting the template before it is created enforces the PRODUCTION BOUNDARY, because no unauthorized artifact ever comes into existence, while reclaiming after the refusal closes only the LEAK -- the directory still exists for the window between creation and reclamation, and a crash or a failed removal in that window puts it back. A control over either repair must pin the refusal to the shape arm (its detail begins 'the directory minted at ', not 'mktemp could not mint a scratch from template ') AND assert the directory exists at the moment of refusal; otherwise it passes because mktemp failed first, which is the wrong reason. The red control runs on a template under a world-writable disposable parent (measured on the runtime box: /var/tmp is 1777 drwxrwxrwt, so uid 1000 can create there), and never under /var/lib/gunbc -- the row would otherwise prove its point by committing the harm it records.",

    "CONSUMPTION: the derived gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.roster recurring_failure_mode_roster includes this per-class declaration, and the prose of test.claim.roadmap.roadmap_event_carrier_fixture cites this row by @ref where it once deferred the obligation to a promised future pull request.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
